From: Unlatch <noreply@github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntfs_readdir: fix index bitmap bit offset when resuming

When ntfs_readdir() resumes at a *pos inside the index allocation, it reads
the index bitmap starting at byte (bmp_pos >> 3) but tests bits from 0, not
from bmp_pos & 7. Every in-use test after that is shifted by up to seven
blocks: used blocks can be skipped (missing entries) and blocks past the end
of $INDEX_ALLOCATION can be read (EIO "Failed to read index block").
A walk from *pos = 0 is aligned, so only resumed walks are affected.

Also stop at the end of $INDEX_ALLOCATION rather than the end of the bitmap,
whose last byte can describe blocks that do not exist.

Found by Unlatch's paged readdir test (Step 04) on a 10,000-entry directory.
---
--- a/libntfs-3g/dir.c	2026-11-01 13:21:38
+++ b/libntfs-3g/dir.c	2026-11-01 13:21:38
@@ -1313,7 +1313,12 @@
 		goto err_out;
 	}
 
-	bmp_buf_pos = 0;
+	/*
+	 * The buffer starts at byte (bmp_pos >> 3), so the first bit to test is
+	 * bmp_pos & 7, not 0. Starting at 0 misaligns every test when resuming
+	 * from a *pos in the middle of a bitmap byte (Unlatch patch 0001).
+	 */
+	bmp_buf_pos = bmp_pos & 7;
 	/* If the index block is not in use find the next one that is. */
 	while (!(bmp[bmp_buf_pos >> 3] & (1 << (bmp_buf_pos & 7)))) {
 find_next_index_buffer:
@@ -1323,6 +1328,9 @@
 		if (bmp_pos >> 3 >= bmp_na->data_size)
 			goto EOD;
 		ia_pos = bmp_pos << index_block_size_bits;
+		/* Bits past the last index block are not blocks (patch 0001). */
+		if (ia_pos >= i_size)
+			goto EOD;
 		if (bmp_buf_pos >> 3 < bmp_buf_size)
 			continue;
 		/* Read next chunk from the index bitmap. */
